From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 09:05:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Take the login value from the "Telegram" chat whatever the
 case of its name

The value asked for in the "Telegram" conversation was taken only from a
message sent to a buddy named exactly "Telegram". A front-end may hand the
name back in another case: webOS's IM transport lowercases who a message
came from, so the prompt is stored as from "telegram" and the Messaging
app replies to "telegram". That reply did not match, went out as an
ordinary message, failed with "User not found", and the login waited for
a code that never arrived, asking again each time the connection timed
out.

The chat is now matched with g_ascii_strcasecmp(). That this could take
a message meant for a user with that display name or @username, such as
Telegram's own @telegram, matters as little as before: the match applies
only while a login value is waited for, when no user is known yet.

A test sends the code to "telegram" and sees it taken.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://github.com/UnitedMarsupials/tdlib-purple/pull/47]
---
 td-client.cpp       |  5 ++++-
 test/login-test.cpp | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/td-client.cpp b/td-client.cpp
index 88ec149..a02abf7 100644
--- a/td-client.cpp
+++ b/td-client.cpp
@@ -2852,7 +2852,10 @@ int PurpleTdClient::sendMessage(const char *buddyName, const char *message, int6
     // Auth-input fallback: while waiting for a login code, 2FA password or display name (asked
     // for through the "Telegram" chat because there is no request UI), take a message sent to that
     // chat as the value instead of sending it to anyone. Messages to anyone else go as usual.
-    if ((m_authInputPending != AuthInput::None) && !g_strcmp0(buddyName, AUTH_INPUT_BUDDY)) {
+    // The chat is matched regardless of case: a front-end may hand back the name it was shown in
+    // another case (webOS stores and replies to "telegram"), and no Telegram user goes by it.
+    if ((m_authInputPending != AuthInput::None) && buddyName &&
+        !g_ascii_strcasecmp(buddyName, AUTH_INPUT_BUDDY)) {
         // OTR/other plugins may inject non-code messages; ignore those.
         if (message && g_str_has_prefix(message, "?OTR"))
             return -1;
diff --git a/test/login-test.cpp b/test/login-test.cpp
index 099ef65..9c001c1 100644
--- a/test/login-test.cpp
+++ b/test/login-test.cpp
@@ -713,6 +713,22 @@ TEST_F(LoginThroughChatTest, AnswersOnlyFromTelegramChat)
                                              PURPLE_MESSAGE_ERROR, 0));
 }
 
+// A front-end may reply to the chat under its name in another case: webOS lowercases who a message
+// came from, and so answers "telegram"
+TEST_F(LoginThroughChatTest, AnswerToChatInAnotherCase)
+{
+    loginUntilCode();
+    prpl.verifyEvents(
+        NewConversationEvent(PURPLE_CONV_TYPE_IM, account, "Telegram"),
+        ServGotImEvent(connection, "Telegram", codePrompt, PURPLE_MESSAGE_RECV, 0)
+    );
+
+    ASSERT_LT(0, send("telegram", "12345"));
+    tgl.verifyRequest(checkAuthenticationCode("12345"));
+
+    finishLogin();
+}
+
 TEST_F(LoginThroughChatTest, NotLoggedWhileWaiting)
 {
     loginUntilCode();
